# English captions (seconds in the ORIGINAL recording NAF003.mp3). *word* = shown in gold.
# Cleaned from the TurboScribe translation for the Shorts. Lines in “quotes” are read from the book Nizam-ul-Amal-e-Fuqara.
# The full dars needs the rest of the transcript (from 18:06) before its captions are complete.
CAPTIONS = [
    (0.39, 54.12, "There is no taqwa in me; I am the lowest of all. You are all good. Now we are to learn about *Taqwa*: page thirteen. A'udhu billahi min ash-shaytan ir-rajeem. Bismillah ir-Rahman ir-Raheem."),
    (55.48, 72.42, 'The topic is *Taqwa*. “Remember: Taqwa begins with *repentance*.”'),
    (72.42, 87.51, '“Taqwa is to keep away from everything that *harms* you.”'),
    (98.61, 121.15, "“Taqwa is to eat *less*, sleep *less*, speak *less*, to weep over one's own wrongs, and to overlook the faults of others.”"),
    (124.56, 128.91, 'What else is there but *humility*? Allahu Akbar.'),
    (128.91, 150.99, 'Now we must look within: are these things in us, or are we guilty of the opposite? Then we must try to *get rid* of them.'),
    (153.81, 179.55, "Taqwa is to keep away from all that is harmful: to eat less, sleep less, speak less, to *weep* over one's own faults, and to *overlook* the faults of others."),
    (180.13, 189.29, '“A *Sufi* is the son of *time*.” The past is gone.'),
    (189.29, 204.09, 'The future is unknown. Now, what do you have in your hands? Only your *present* state, this moment.'),
    (205.11, 212.19, "When the time comes, the work comes with it. If you put today's work off to tomorrow, when will you do tomorrow's work?"),
    (213.97, 236.03, 'Tell me: after becoming a murid, what troubles does Shaytan put us in? This should not happen. That is why I keep saying: read this book. It teaches not only the murid, but the *shaykh* too.'),
    (239.05, 243.19, 'So whatever you have to do, do it *today*.'),
    (245.62, 272.92, 'What is Taqwa? To keep away from everything that *harms* you: from neglecting what you must do, and from committing sins.'),
    (320.48, 329.64, '“A person has many needs and many aims. To think that your own efforts alone will fulfil them is wrong.”'),
    (332.08, 361.64, '“To fulfil your needs, you must *trust* in Allah”: to turn to Him, to ask Him for help, to hope that Allah will give me. This connection with Allah is a very special thing.'),
    (364.07, 374.39, '“To rely on Allah for your needs: this is called *Tawakkul*.”'),
    (374.52, 402.93, 'This is *Tawakkul*. Some of you may know it, some may not, but Hazrat explains every point here. These words carry deep meaning and need reflection: to rely on Allah for your needs, this is Tawakkul.'),
    (404.21, 427.30, "“*Not working* is not tawakkul.” “I'm sitting here; Allah will do whatever He wants.” How can that be? You must work. Do something!"),
    (427.97, 438.25, '“That is *seeking comfort*: wasting your hands and feet, the powers Allah has given you.”'),
    (438.95, 456.71, "That is great *disrespect* to Allah. Whatever Allah has given you, use it. If you don't, you are disrespecting Allah."),
    (457.53, 467.07, 'MashaAllah. It is a small topic, but see how it changes a whole *life*.'),
    (469.98, 490.60, 'Now page fourteen: *Qurb-e-Faraiz* and *Qurb-e-Nawafil*, nearness to Allah through the obligatory and through the voluntary.'),
    (608.45, 618.40, '“Whatever work you have to do, do it by *asking* Allah.” This is *Istikhara*.'),
    (618.55, 633.29, '“O Allah, I want to do such-and-such work, I want to write such-and-such a book. Grant me the ability to do it.”'),
    (635.09, 646.65, 'After this you feel *peace* within. “Yes, now I am at peace”: it means you have been given leave for this work. This is Istikhara.'),
    (648.29, 662.11, "For a daughter's marriage too: “Will this relationship be right for our child?” Ask *Allah*. This is Istikhara."),
    (665.13, 676.41, "Very few people know this. I don't even tell it, or else they will reach great heights and I'll be left behind!"),
    (680.95, 711.47, 'Before, I told you how to make murids. Now I am telling you how to do Istikhara. Pay attention: “O Allah, let me know whether I should do it or not.” Whatever you do, *ask Allah* first.'),
    (713.67, 776.53, "To do a good work by your own intention is nearness through *nawafil*; to work after Allah's command is nearness through *faraiz*. To leave all your affairs to Allah is *Tawakkul*. MashaAllah! What teachings our Urdu holds: words no one hears or reads any more, yet they contain the whole of religion."),
    (783.49, 811.01, 'Whatever work you have to do, ask Allah first: this is *Istikhara*. Doing good by your own intention is Qurb-e-Nawafil; working after the command is Qurb-e-Faraiz; leaving all your affairs to Allah is Tawakkul.'),
    (839.23, 848.39, 'This is a very important matter in *Sufism*.'),
    (850.41, 864.15, 'A person stayed up the *whole night* in prayer, and went to sleep just before morning.'),
    (865.11, 893.49, "And he *missed* the Fajr prayer. He couldn't pray it. The fard prayer is Allah's command; he had to keep it, and he missed it."),
    (897.07, 915.35, 'Another person also stayed up the night, and in the morning he prayed the two rakats of *fard*. Do you see?'),
    (917.73, 931.00, 'Clearly, his *two rakats* are better than a hundred rakats of the night vigil, because they are under the *command*.'),
    (931.24, 938.52, "Command. Order. Whose? Allah's. Allah's command is to pray the fard before sunrise."),
    (940.72, 958.32, "He prayed the sunnah and the fard. If there isn't time for the sunnah, leave the sunnah and pray the *fard*."),
    (961.22, 1001.92, "It is by Allah's command: it belongs to *Qurb-e-Faraiz*, nearness through the obligatory. A hundred rakats of one's own choosing, *Qurb-e-Nawafil*, cannot compare with these two rakats."),
    (1003.77, 1080.50, "What should we do so that all our work falls under Qurb-e-Faraiz? Before any work, recall the verse of the *Qur'an* that commands it; then the work is done under Allah's command. And there are two kinds of command: the command of *approval*, what is acceptable, and the command of *test*, what is forbidden."),
    (1087.46, 1146.52, "The command to *do* is clear, and so is the command to *refrain*. Act according to the command, and keep away from what is forbidden. Because we act under the command, according to Allah's order, these deeds enter *Qurb-e-Faraiz*."),
    (1146.52, 1199.66, "For example, before eating we read *Kulu wa-shrabu min rizqillah*: eat and drink of Allah's provision. Bismillah ir-Rahman ir-Raheem, and then we begin. First recite the verse, then begin to eat."),
    (1199.66, 1253.32, "When we want to do the dhikr of the Kalima, *La ilaha illa Allah*, we first read *Fa'lam annahu la ilaha illa Allah*: know that there is no god but Allah."),
    (1253.48, 1318.38, "Before durood sharif we first read *Innallaha wa mala'ikatahu yusalluna 'ala-n-Nabi*: Allah and His angels send blessings upon the Prophet ﷺ; O you who believe, send blessings upon him and greet him with peace. This is a lesson to remember."),
    (1320.70, 1370.00, 'Everyone should recite it, because this verse carries a *command*: Allah and His angels send blessings upon the Prophet ﷺ. O you who believe, send blessings upon him.'),
    (1398.68, 1524.70, "*Allahumma salli 'ala Sayyidina Muhammad* ﷺ."),
    (1530.58, 1566.84, 'Some people do everything by *asking*: they read *Allahumma khir li wakhtar li*. After istikhara, at first the heart settles on nothing; after a few days the answer comes from Allah. They act only on the order.'),
    (1567.56, 1622.24, 'You watch me: what do I do? Some come to me to become *murids* and ask what to read. I tell them: read this. As soon as he reads, his condition changes completely; even in America, his troubles go and his work is done.'),
    (1626.02, 1661.74, "You try too; when you become able, Allah will bless you as well. This came from his *company*: he kept following the orders, and that is why we have Allah's grace."),
    (1664.02, 1702.56, "I don't make anyone a murid until I get *permission* from Sarkar. When I ask, he says: do it. Sometimes he says no, and then I send that person back. So ask first, then act."),
    (1707.94, 1722.56, 'In making a murid we are only a *medium*: in truth he has become the murid of Sarkar and entered his service.'),
    (1738.66, 1777.52, 'Remember the *obligations* and the *prohibitions*: some things must be done, some must be avoided. When to do istikhara and when not to, I am telling you that too.'),
    (1777.86, 1834.64, 'For what is obligatory or forbidden, *istikhara* is not done: these are commands brought by the sinless Prophet ﷺ, who told us what is halal and what is haram. If it is obligatory, do it; if it is haram, keep away.'),
    (1836.58, 1894.42, "*Present your kashf to the Qur'an and Hadith.* Allahu Akbar, how can I teach you all this? You are receiving a very good education, MashaAllah."),
    (1894.42, 1941.60, "It is as if Sarkar is addressing me with this command: present your kashf to the Qur'an and Hadith. If it agrees, act on it; if it does not, leave it."),
    (1948.90, 1999.40, "If a command is given in *kashf* in the form of the Prophet ﷺ, it is binding, because Shaytan cannot take the form of the Prophet ﷺ. Remember: what is shown in kashf is shown in *'Alam-e-Mithal*, the world of images."),
    (2005.82, 2059.90, "But beware: where there is a clear text (*mansoos*), one's own judgement (*ijtihad*) is not accepted. If a kashf does not agree with the Qur'an and Hadith, leave it; if it agrees, accept it."),
    (2060.06, 2133.67, 'If the command comes from one who is *Jalali*, you will not have the courage to refuse. Obey at once, and say: My Lord, I have erred; forgive me. This is an advanced matter, understood only after crossing many stages. If you must act on such a command, *repent* at once and read Istighfar.'),
    (2134.51, 2207.66, 'If one of our Murshids, *Qadeer Piya Sarkar*, Khwaja Mian Sarkar or Khwaja Qadeer Nawaz Sarkar, gives such a command, it is the way of the *Shuyukh*. Some ignorant people say a command is acceptable only if the Prophet ﷺ himself gives it, in the form he had at forty. *Beware* of such people.'),
    (2207.66, 2279.99, 'Some say: if the disciple is *sincere*, pure of heart, he receives the command with full presence.'),
    (2280.75, 2351.65, "*Wa-lladhina jahadu fina la-nahdiyannahum subulana*: those who strive for Us, We will surely guide them on Our paths. This is Allah's promise. Sometimes a person is aware, yet his hands and feet are not in his *control*: he means to go to one place and reaches another."),
    (2352.39, 2369.73, "Sometimes words come out of his mouth, or his hands act, without his thinking first; he understands only afterwards. These are the ways of Allah: keeping him on the *straight path* is Allah's work."),
    (2395.19, 2468.23, 'Let me explain more clearly. In a gathering of *sama*, when it reaches its height, you cannot stay in your place. I see everyone, the qawwal with the harmonium and the tabla, yet I am not in my own control.'),
    (2468.99, 2527.63, 'When there was qawwali in *Malakpet*, a Pathan used to come and sit in the gathering with a *pin*. Whoever seemed lost in ecstasy, he would prick with it, to test whether the state was real.'),
    (2527.63, 2602.47, 'One should never be *arrogant*; stay humble and courteous. Remember: in sama a person may mean to go one way and end up another, and words may suddenly come out of his mouth.'),
    (2603.55, 2638.38, 'Afterwards he says: Oh, I said this, I did that! He does not act by thinking; only afterwards does he understand. These are the ways of Allah: it is Allah who makes him do all this.'),
    (2656.60, 2729.52, 'Among ordinary people, some have *wishes* in their hearts and receive them from the *Darbar-e-Ghausiya*: “What is lacking in Your lane, Maula? The world is in Your lane, and the Hereafter too.” They are Qadri too, but near through *nawafil*. And some have no wishes at all: they ask neither for good nor to be kept from evil.'),
    (2730.46, 2801.43, "Before Allah they become *ka'l-mayyiti fi yadil-ghassal*: like the dead body in the hands of the one who washes it. The washer does as he wills: washes it, turns it, sits it up."),
    (2843.81, 2918.27, "Or like a nursing *baby* in its mother's lap: she feeds him, washes his clothes, cares for him. These are the Qadris who are near through *faraiz*. Remember one thing from the Ahadith."),
    (2918.27, 2985.52, 'For those near through nawafil, Allah becomes their hands and feet: He fulfils their needs. Those near through *faraiz* have no wishes of their own; they become the hands and feet through which Allah does His work. They live without desire, and there is *blessing*, peace and honour in what they do.'),
    (2992.74, 3023.58, "May Allah bless you all. MashaAllah, it is Allah's grace. Today's date is 9-10-2011. Wasil Mian, read the naat."),
]
